AI generated proposal to improve performance for @pullfrog to evaluate:

$ZodObjectJIT.init calls $ZodObject.init(inst, def) and then builds a second util.cached(() => normalizeDef(def)) over the same def — the base one's is a local const in the other initializer, so it just isn't reachable. $ZodObject.init now publishes the box it built and $ZodObjectJIT.init adopts it when the def matches, falling back to its own otherwise.

Impact: −368 B per object schema, −2.44% memory on a realistic catalogue. Construction z.object() −9.2%, discriminated unions −10.2%. Under jitless both boxes are actually read, so normalizeDef really did run twice — a 3-key object drops −14.3% there. Parse unchanged. Bundle +11 B gzip classic, +14 B mini.

Note this overlaps with the util.cached change (both target the same boxes): together they give −4.33% rather than the −6.00% their separate figures suggest.

