AI generated proposal to improve performance for @pullfrog to evaluate:

z.string(), z.literal() and z.enum() each compile a RegExp at construction, but _zod.pattern is only ever read by z.templateLiteral(), toJSONSchema() and union pattern derivation. Literal and enum now build it lazily through the existing defineLazyInternal, which also drops the escapeRegex/map/join work from the constructor. For strings the pattern stays eager but an unconstrained bag returns one shared regex instead of a fresh one, and the [...patterns].pop() array is gone.

Impact: construction z.enum() −44.3%, z.literal() −24.2%, z.string() −25.5%, discriminated unions −15.9%. Memory −1.26% on a realistic catalogue (−88 B per string schema, −91 B per literal, −100 B per enum). Parse unchanged. Bundle +63 B gzip classic, +49 B mini — the string half accounts for nearly all of that; the literal/enum half is free on mini because defineLazyInternal is already there.

