**Not a vulnerability report** — no prototype pollution occurs; the existing defense works. This is a question about `.strict()`'s reporting semantics that falls out of where that defense sits.

### What happens

`z.object(...).strict()` (and `z.strictObject`) reports every unknown input key as `unrecognized_keys` — except an own `__proto__` key, which is silently accepted and silently dropped from the parse output. So strict mode flags every unknown key except the one an attacker would actually send, and downstream code that inspects `parsed.data` never learns the key was there.

### Repro (zod 4.4.3, fresh install, Node 24)

```js
import { z } from 'zod';

const strict = z.object({ name: z.string() }).strict();

// JSON.parse creates an OWN "__proto__" property — the wire reality for any JSON body.
const wire = JSON.parse('{"name":"a","__proto__":{"admin":true}}');
console.log(Object.getOwnPropertyNames(wire)); // [ 'name', '__proto__' ]

const res = strict.safeParse(wire);
console.log(res.success);                                          // true   (expected: false)
console.log(Object.getOwnPropertyNames(res.data));                 // [ 'name' ]  — dropped
console.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(res.data) === Object.prototype); // true — defense intact 👍

// Control: any other unknown key is reported as documented.
console.log(strict.safeParse(JSON.parse('{"name":"a","other":1}')).success); // false, unrecognized_keys
```

### Where it comes from

The pollution guard in `handleCatchall` skips `__proto__` **before** the `unrecognized.push(key)` branch:

https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/blob/912f0f51b0ced654d0069741e7160834dca742ee/packages/zod/src/v4/core/schemas.ts#L1870-L1878

```ts
for (const key in input) {
  // skip __proto__ so it can't replace the result prototype via the
  // assignment setter on the plain {} we build into
  if (key === "__proto__") continue;   // ← also exempts it from the strict check below
  if (keySet.has(key)) continue;
  if (t === "never") {
    unrecognized.push(key);
    continue;
  }
```

The comment justifies not *copying* the key into the result (clearly right, please keep it). It doesn't say the key should also be exempt from strict's *reporting* — that looks like a side effect of where the `continue` sits. `z.record` has the same silent skip (schemas.ts#L2957 (same permalink base)).

### Why it matters

For anyone using `.strict()` as a trust-boundary contract ("a body carrying anything unexpected is refused loudly, never stripped silently"), this is the one input where the contract silently doesn't hold — and because the key is also absent from the output, a downstream check can't catch it either. We hit this in an e-signature API where a rejected-extra-keys guarantee is load-bearing; our unit tests proved the downstream authority rejects a smuggled `__proto__`, while the deployed pipeline never showed it that key at all. (Worked around with an own-key check beside the parse.)

### Suggested fix, if unintended

Report before skipping — keeps the copy-defense byte-for-byte identical:

```ts
if (key === "__proto__") {
  if (t === "never") unrecognized.push(key);
  continue;
}
```

Happy to send a PR with tests if you'd take this. And if the current behavior is intentional, a line in the `.strict()` docs naming the exemption would let integrators know to pin own-keys themselves.

## Summary

When exporting JSON Schema from a schema that contains a `bigint` default value, Zod throws before the `override` callback is invoked.

This makes it impossible to normalize `bigint` output (for example to `{ type: "integer", format: "int64" }`) inside `override` for schemas that also use `.default(0n)`.

## Minimal reproduction

```ts
import { z } from 'zod'

const schema = z.object({
  startAt: z.coerce.bigint().optional().default(0n)
})

const jsonSchema = schema.toJSONSchema({
  io: 'input',
  unrepresentable: 'any',
  override: ({ zodSchema, jsonSchema }) => {
      if (zodSchema._zod.def.type === 'bigint') {
        jsonSchema.type = 'integer'
        jsonSchema.format = 'int64'
      }
  }
})

console.log(jsonSchema)
```

## Actual behavior

A `TypeError` is thrown:

```txt
TypeError: Do not know how to serialize a BigInt
    at JSON.stringify (<anonymous>)
    at Module.defaultProcessor (.../zod/v4/core/json-schema-processors.js:464:36)
```

The failure occurs while processing defaults, before `override` can run for the relevant subtree.

## Expected behavior

One of the following should happen:

1. `override` is applied before default serialization for the affected node, or
2. `defaultProcessor` handles `bigint` defaults without throwing (for example by converting to string), or
3. a documented and supported option exists to control default serialization for `bigint`.

## Why this is a problem

`bigint` support can be represented in JSON Schema by projects with custom conventions (for example `integer` + `int64`).

Current behavior prevents customization for valid schemas that include `bigint` defaults because export fails too early.

## Workaround used locally

Temporarily patch schema internals to coerce `bigint` defaults to strings during `toJSONSchema`, then restore original values afterward.

```
function patchBigIntDefaultsForJsonSchema(rootSchema: unknown): () => void {
  const seen = new Set<object>()
  const restorations: Array<() => void> = []

  const visitSchema = (candidate: unknown): void => {
    if (!candidate || typeof candidate !== 'object' || seen.has(candidate)) {
      return
    }

    seen.add(candidate)

    const internalZod = Reflect.get(candidate, '_zod') as
      | { def?: Record<string, unknown> }
      | undefined
    const definition = internalZod?.def
    const valuesToVisit: Array<unknown> = []

    if (definition) {
      const defaultValueDescriptor = Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(
        definition,
        'defaultValue'
      )
      const defaultValue = definition.defaultValue

      if (
        typeof defaultValue === 'bigint' &&
        defaultValueDescriptor?.configurable
      ) {
        Object.defineProperty(definition, 'defaultValue', {
          value: defaultValue.toString(),
          writable: true,
          enumerable: defaultValueDescriptor.enumerable,
          configurable: true
        })
        restorations.push(() => {
          Object.defineProperty(
            definition,
            'defaultValue',
            defaultValueDescriptor
          )
        })
      }

      valuesToVisit.push(...Object.values(definition))
    } else {
      valuesToVisit.push(...Object.values(candidate as Record<string, unknown>))
    }

    for (const value of valuesToVisit) {
      if (Array.isArray(value)) {
        for (const item of value) {
          visitSchema(item)
        }
      } else {
        visitSchema(value)
      }
    }
  }

  visitSchema(rootSchema)

  return () => {
    for (const restore of restorations) {
      restore()
    }
  }
}
```

This is brittle and relies on internal implementation details.

## Environment

- Node: 24.4.1
- Zod: v4 (observed in `zod/v4/core/json-schema-processors.js`)
- Reproduced in a TypeScript test using `schema.toJSONSchema(...)`

Code declaring tools.
Zod v4.

parameters: z.object({
          task: z.string().describe("Complete task description to achieve the user goal, understandable by another LLM.")
}),


execute: async (input) => {
console.log("task: ", JSON.stringify(input));
}

output as follows:
task: {"description": "..."}

expecting
task: {"task":"..."}

### Description

For a `z.tuple([...])` with **no** `.rest()`, `.parse()` / `.safeParse()` strictly rejects arrays that are longer or shorter than the tuple. `.toJSONSchema()` does not reproduce this constraint faithfully in **any** target:

| target | positional item validation | length bound (`maxItems`/closed) |
|---|---|---|
| `draft-7` | ✅ correct | ❌ missing — extra trailing items pass unchecked |
| `draft-2020-12` | ✅ correct | ❌ missing (`items: false` never emitted) |
| `openapi-3.0` | ❌ **lost** — collapses to `items: { anyOf: [...] }`, so element *order* is no longer enforced | ✅ `maxItems`/`minItems` present |

No target matches `.parse()`'s accept set: `draft-7`/`draft-2020-12` leave the tail unbounded; `openapi-3.0` bounds the length but loses per-index typing (verified with reordered/duplicate elements).

This matters for consumers like ajv (e.g. via Fastify) that validate only against the emitted schema, not zod's runtime.

### Reproduction

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

const schema = z.tuple([
  z.string(),
  z.string(),
  z.object({ lang: z.string() }),
]);

schema.safeParse(["a", "b", { lang: "en" }, "extra"]).success;
// -> false (runtime: "too_big", maximum: 3)

console.log(JSON.stringify(schema.toJSONSchema({ target: "draft-7" })));
// {"type":"array","items":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"string"},
//  {"type":"object","properties":{"lang":{"type":"string"}},"required":["lang"],"additionalProperties":false}]}
// -> no "maxItems", no "additionalItems": false

console.log(JSON.stringify(schema.toJSONSchema({ target: "openapi-3.0" })));
// {"type":"array","items":{"anyOf":[{"type":"string"},{"type":"string"},
//  {"type":"object","properties":{"lang":{"type":"string"}},"required":["lang"],"additionalProperties":false}]},
//  "minItems":3,"maxItems":3}
// -> length is bounded, but positional typing is gone:
//    ["a","a","a"] now validates, even though schema.safeParse(["a","a","a"]) rejects it
//    (index 2 must be the `{lang}` object, not a string)
```

Feeding the `draft-7` schema to ajv, `["a","b",{lang:"en"},null]` (unvalidated 4th element) passes. Feeding the `openapi-3.0` schema to ajv, `["a","a","a"]` (no `{lang}` object anywhere) also passes. Both diverge from `schema.safeParse()`.

### Root cause (from reading `v4/core/*`)

- `schemas.ts` (`$ZodTuple.init`): when `def.rest == null`, the parser enforces `input.length > items.length` directly … independent of `schema._zod.bag`.
- `json-schema-processors.ts` (`tupleProcessor`):
  - `draft-7`/`draft-2020-12`: only closes the tail (`additionalItems: false` / `items: false`) when `def.rest` is set; otherwise nothing marks the schema as closed.
  - `openapi-3.0`: correctly infers `maxItems`/`minItems` from `prefixItems.length` when `!rest`, but represents `items` as `{ anyOf: prefixItems }`, which is positionally unsound (OpenAPI 3.0's `items` only accepts a single schema, so this is the best available approximation, but it silently discards per-index typing).

The two families of targets fail in complementary but both incorrect ways; there's no existing target that gets both right.

### Expected behavior

For a `.rest()`-less tuple:
- `draft-7`: `additionalItems: false`
- `draft-2020-12`: `items: false`
- `openapi-3.0`: ideally reject/warn that a closed tuple can't be represented losslessly in OpenAPI 3.0 `items`, rather than silently producing a schema with different accept semantics (or emit `minItems`/`maxItems` *and* document that positional constraints are unenforceable in that target).

### Environment

- zod: 4.4.3
- Node.js: v24.18.0

`.partial()` wraps every field in `ZodOptional`, even when a field already accepts absent input. Runtime behavior is correct, but the inferred types become inaccurate.

### Already-optional fields are double-wrapped

```ts
const schema = z.object({ a: z.string().optional() }).partial();
type A = typeof schema.shape.a;
// ZodOptional<ZodOptional<ZodString>>
```

Repeated `.partial()` calls keep nesting `ZodOptional` unnecessarily.

### Defaulted fields get broadened to `undefined`

```ts
const schema = z.object({ foo: z.string().default("foo") }).partial();
type Foo = z.output<typeof schema>;
// { foo?: string | undefined }
```

But `foo` is never `undefined` after parsing:

```ts
schema.parse({});
// { foo: "foo" }
```

The inferred output should reflect that.

### This is a type-only issue

I'm not proposing any runtime changes. Defaults applying through `.partial()` are intentional (#5235, #5315, #5642, #4799), and `.required().partial()` remains the escape hatch for disabling them. This is only about making the inferred types match the values `.partial()` produces.

Environment

- Zod version: 4.4.3

When defining a Record with enums as keys and values with defaults, then zod is able to complete the missing keys with the default values in the parsed result:
```ts
import z from "zod"

const schema = z.record(z.enum(["UP", "DOWN"]), z.string().default("unknown"))

type Input = z.input<typeof schema>

// @ts-ignore
const input: Input = {
    UP: "somewhere"
}

console.log(z.parse(schema, input))
```
output:
```
{ UP: 'somewhere', DOWN: 'unknown' }
```

The only issue is that the inferred type `Input` is:
```ts
type Input = {
    UP: string | undefined;
    DOWN: string | undefined;
}
```
which requires using `// @ts-ignore` when defining partial inputs, otherwise typescript complains with:
```
error TS2741: Property 'DOWN' is missing in type '{ UP: string; }' but required in type 'Record<"UP" | "DOWN", string | undefined>
```

Assuming it is intended for zod to generate the missing fields in the output, then it would be amazing if the inferred `z.input` type to be Partial. For example:
```ts
type Input = Partial<{
    UP: string | undefined;
    DOWN: string | undefined;
}>
```

**Repro** (zod 4.4.3, also present on `main`):

```ts
import * as z from "zod";

let calls = 0;
const codec = z.codec(z.iso.datetime(), z.date(), {
  decode: (iso) => new Date(iso),
  encode: (date) => {
    calls++;
    return date.toISOString();
  },
});

z.encode(z.object({ at: codec }), { at: new Date() });
// calls === 1 ✅

calls = 0;
z.encode(z.object({ at: codec }).refine(() => true), { at: new Date() });
// calls === 2 ❌ — one checked ancestor doubles it

calls = 0;
const inner = z.object({ at: codec }).refine(() => true);
z.encode(z.object({ nested: inner }).refine(() => true), { nested: { at: new Date() } });
// calls === 3 ❌ — multiplies with nesting depth

calls = 0;
z.encode(z.object({ items: z.array(z.object({ at: codec })).min(1) }), {
  items: [{ at: new Date() }, { at: new Date() }],
});
// calls === 4 ❌ — twice per element
```

### Cause

In the backward direction, `$ZodType`'s `run` performs a canary parse with `skipChecks: true` (shielding domain-side checks from invalid values), then — after this node's checks — re-parses the value in full (`handleCanaryResult` in `packages/zod/src/v4/core/schemas.ts`). `skipChecks` skips *checks*, but not *transforms*: every codec `encode` and pipe transform in the subtree executes once per pass, so each check-bearing ancestor adds a full extra execution of the subtree below it.

### Impact

- Side-effectful or non-idempotent `encode` functions (metrics, ID allocation, logging) fire 2–N times per `encode()` call.
- Expensive encodes (encryption, compression, big payloads) pay 2x+ wall time whenever any ancestor carries a `.refine()`/`.min()`/etc.
- A related reporting inconsistency: because the canary skips checks and aborts on the first fatal issue, continuable check issues that `parse()` reports are masked in `encode()` for the same schema and value:

```ts
const schema = z.object({ a: z.string().min(5), b: z.number() }).refine(() => true);
z.safeParse(schema, { a: "abc", b: "nan" }).error.issues;
// [too_small at ["a"], invalid_type at ["b"]]
z.safeEncode(schema, { a: "abc", b: "nan" }).error.issues;
// [invalid_type at ["b"]] — too_small masked
```

### Suggested fix

The second parse is only needed when the node's **own checks can mutate the value** — and `$ZodCheckOverwrite` is the designated mutating check family (`trim`, `toLowerCase`, `toUpperCase`, `normalize`, `slugify` all build on it). So: run the canary **with** descendant checks enabled; when the node's own checks contain no overwrite-family check, reuse the canary's value and issues and elide the second parse. Overwrite-bearing nodes keep the current two-pass flow, and abort semantics are unchanged (an invalid value still returns before this node's checks execute).

Working implementation: [`drudolf/zod@encode-canary-reuse`](https://github.com/drudolf/zod/tree/encode-canary-reuse), single commit [`154494f`](https://github.com/drudolf/zod/commit/154494fb54670e315dcc0f83122493c504f5166e). All 3,821 existing tests pass, plus 10 new tests in `codec.test.ts` covering encode-once semantics, descendant-check failures under checked parents (sync/async), issue ordering, overwrite parents, and unidirectional transforms.

Measured: ~1.9x faster encode on check-dense codec schemas (µbench, output verified byte-identical); +4–10% on realistic mixed schemas in fastify serialization benchmarks.

Two deliberate behavioral notes:

1. An aborted canary now also reports continuable descendant check issues — this aligns backward reporting with forward (fixes the masking shown above).
2. A custom check that mutates `payload.value` without being an overwrite check would lose its mutation in the reuse path (previously the re-parse picked it up). `.overwrite()` is the designated mutation mechanism; runtime value-identity detection was rejected as unsound for in-place reference mutation.

Happy to open a PR from the branch if this direction is acceptable.

## Description

`z.iso.datetime()` rejects ISO 8601 datetime strings using the expanded year representation (leading `+`/`-` sign with a 6-digit year).

## Reproduction

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

const schema = z.iso.datetime();

const date1 = new Date("9999-12-31T00:00:00.000Z").toISOString();
console.log(date1); // "9999-12-31T00:00:00.000Z"
console.log(schema.safeParse(date1)); // { success: true, data: '9999-12-31T00:00:00.000Z' }

const date2 = new Date("+010000-01-01T00:00:00.000Z").toISOString();
console.log(date2); // "+010000-01-01T00:00:00.000Z"
console.log(schema.safeParse(date2));
// { success: false, error: ZodError: [{ code: "invalid_format", format: "datetime", message: "Invalid ISO datetime", ... }] }
```

## Expected behavior

Both `safeParse` calls should succeed, since both inputs are valid outputs of `Date#toISOString()`.

Per the [docs](https://zod.dev/api?id=iso-datetimes#iso-datetimes):

> The `z.iso.datetime()` method enforces ISO 8601;

ISO 8601 permits an expanded year representation (leading `+`/`-` sign with a 6-digit year) for years outside 0000–9999.

Rejecting a valid ISO 8601 expanded-year string is a mismatch between the documented behavior and the implementation.

## Actual behavior

The year-10000 case fails with `Invalid ISO datetime`.

