I have a test suite that was used to ensure a schema validation wrapper class functioned the same when we switched from `convict` to `zod`. One of the tests calls `superRefine()` on an optional field of an object, expecting the function to throw and add an issue to `ctx`, even if the field is not defined on the object.

Here is a simplified example. In 4.2.1 the following schema would throw if we parse an object where the field is not set. In 4.3.0 schema.parse() no longer throws.
```js
const schema = z.object({
    field: z.any().optional().superRefine((val, ctx) => {
        ctx.addIssue({
            code: 'custom',
            message: 'This validation should fail',
        });
    })
});

const result = schema.parse({}); // throws in 4.2.1 only, not in 4.3.0
```

If I set field to undefined the schema will throw:
```js
const result = schema.parse({ field: undefined }); //throws
```

If I parse the field's schema directly (not wrapped in an object) the error is thrown:
```js
const worksOutsideObject = z.any().optional().superRefine((val, ctx) => {
    ctx.addIssue({
        code: 'custom',
        message: 'This validation should fail',
    });
});

const result = worksOutsideObject.parse(undefined); // throws
```

This works the same for other functions that throw errors, like `refine()` and `check()`.

I think this new behavior makes sense, I just want to confirm that it's intentional and will not change. If so, I can send over a PR to update the documentation if you are interested.

