## Problem

Extending Zod with custom schemas that live under the `z` namespace can break in production builds. The `z` object and sub-namespaces like `z.iso` are frozen ESM module namespaces, so direct property assignment fails:
```typescript
// Works in dev, breaks in prod
z.iso.timezone = () => z.string().check(...);
// TypeError: Cannot assign to read only property
```

The current workarounds (standalone exports, wrapper objects, disabling bundler freezing) all fragment the developer experience.

## Proposal

Adopt a plugin system similar to dayjs:
```typescript
// Plugin definition
import type { PluginFunc } from 'zod/v4';

const cronPlugin: PluginFunc = (z, options) => {
  z.cron = () =>
    z.string().check((ctx) => {
      // validation logic
    });
};

export default cronPlugin;
```
```typescript
// Plugin registration (app entry point)
import { z } from 'zod/v4';
import cronPlugin from 'zod-plugin-cron';

z.extend(cronPlugin);

// Usage
z.cron();
```

### Type Definitions (Plugin Author)
```typescript
import type { PluginFunc, ZodString } from 'zod/v4';

declare const plugin: PluginFunc;
export default plugin;

declare module 'zod/v4' {
  namespace z {
    function cron(): ZodString;

    namespace iso {
      function timezone(): ZodString;
    }
  }
}
```

## Similar Approaches from other Open Source

dayjs uses this exact pattern:
```typescript
import dayjs from 'dayjs';
import duration from 'dayjs/plugin/duration';

dayjs.extend(duration);
dayjs.duration(1, 'hour');
```

Zod already has an internal example of this pattern in `zod/plugin/effect`, which extends prototypes with a `Symbol.for()` guard to prevent double-registration.

## Why This Matters

1. **Bundler-safe** — `z.extend()` can internally manage a mutable registry that the frozen `z` proxies to
2. **Ecosystem growth** — Standardized plugin format encourages community contributions
3. **Discoverability** — Everything stays under `z.`, autocomplete works naturally

