This is using zod 4.1.13 and appears to be caused by this check:

https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/blob/main/packages/zod/src/v4/core/to-json-schema.ts#L828

Because the original `schema` is considered a parent, it is not overridden.

```
export const example = z.union([
  z.string().min(10),
  z.string().min(5)
]);
```

will generate schema like this in `zod@4.1.13`

```
"exampleField": {
  "anyOf":[
    {"type":"string","minLength":10},
    {"type":"string","minLength":5}
  ]
}
```

but in zod v3 it generates:
```
"exampleField": {
  "oneOf":[
    {"type":"string","minLength":10},
    {"type":"string","minLength":5}
  ]
}
```

I think it should use `oneOf` instead of `anyOf`

```
export const example = z.union([
  z.string().min(10),
  z.string().min(5)],
);
```

will generate schema like this in `zod@4.1.13`

```
"exampleField": {
  "anyOf":[
    {"type":"string","minLength":10},
    {"type":"string","minLength":5}
  ]
}
```

but in zod v3 it generates:
```
"exampleField": {
  "oneOf":[
    {"type":"string","minLength":10},
    {"type":"string","minLength":5}
  ]
}
```

I think it should use `oneOf` instead of `anyOf`

When using z.intersection or z.and the typescript types do not match the actual parsing behaviour.

I think the inferred types should match the actual behavior, such that:
- if a type is considered invalid by the typescript compiler, it should produce a parsing error
- if a type is considered valid by the typescript compiler, parsing should also work (except in a few more refined cases than can not be expressed by the typescript type system).

Here are a few examples of where the types and the behavior do not match.
```ts
  const baseSchema = z.strictObject({
    a: z.string(),
    b: z.number(),
  });

  const extensionSchema = z.object({
    c: z.boolean(),
  });

  const laxCombinedSchema = z.object(baseSchema.shape).and(extensionSchema);
  // { a: string; b: number; } & { c: boolean; }
  // but should be { a: string; b: number; } & { c: boolean; } & { [key: string]: any; }
  type TLaxCombinedIn = z.input<typeof laxCombinedSchema>;
  // { a: string; b: number; } & { c: boolean; }
  type TLaxCombinedOut = z.output<typeof laxCombinedSchema>;
  const laxIn: TLaxCombinedIn = {
    a: "foo",
    b: 1,
    c: true,
    d: "anything",
  } as TLaxCombinedIn; // ❌ Type Error: unrecognized key(s), but 'fixed' by adding `as TLaxCombinedIn`
  laxCombinedSchema.parse(laxIn); // ✅ OK

  const combinedSchema = baseSchema.and(extensionSchema);
  // { a: string; b: number; } & { c: boolean; } but should be { [key: string]: never; }
  type TCombinedIn = z.input<typeof combinedSchema>;
  // { a: string; b: number; } & { c: boolean; } but should be { [key: string]: never; }
  type TCombinedOut = z.output<typeof combinedSchema>; 
  const combinedIn: TCombinedIn = { a: "foo", b: 1, c: true }; // ✅ OK though it shouldn't be
  try {
    combinedSchema.parse(combinedIn); // ❌ Error while parsing: Unrecognized key: "c"
  } catch (e) {
    console.error("Error parsing combined schema with extra properties, while no type error.");
  }

  const intersectionSchema = z.intersection(baseSchema, extensionSchema);
  // { a: string; b: number; } & { c: boolean; } but should be { [key: string]: never; }
  type TIntersectionIn = z.input<typeof intersectionSchema>;
  // { a: string; b: number; } & { c: boolean; } but should be { [key: string]: never; }
  type TIntersectionOut = z.output<typeof intersectionSchema>;
  const intersectionIn: TIntersectionIn = { a: "foo", b: 1, c: true }; // ✅ OK though it shouldn't be
  try {
    intersectionSchema.parse(intersectionIn); // ❌ Error while parsing: Unrecognized key: "c"
  } catch (e) {
    console.error("Error parsing intersection schema with extra properties, while no type error.");
  }

  const extensionStrictSchema = z.strictObject(extensionSchema.shape);
  const combinedStrict = baseSchema.and(extensionStrictSchema);
  // { a: string; b: number; } & { c: boolean; } but should be { [key: string]: never; }
  type TCombinedStrictIn = z.input<typeof combinedStrict>;
  // { a: string; b: number; } & { c: boolean; } but should be { [key: string]: never; }
  type TCombinedStrictOut = z.output<typeof combinedStrict>;
  // => same problem as combined, but even worse

  const extendedSchema = baseSchema.extend(extensionStrictSchema.shape);
  extendedSchema.parse({ a: "foo", b: 1, c: true }); // ✅ OK
  console.log("Extended schema parsed successfully with extension.");

  // type TExtendedIn = { a: string; b: number; c: boolean; }
  type TExtendedIn = z.input<typeof extendedSchema>;
  // type TExtendedOut = { a: string; b: number; c: boolean; }
  type TExtendedOut = z.output<typeof extendedSchema>;
  const extendedIn: TExtendedIn = { a: "foo", b: 1, c: true }; // ✅ OK
  const extendedOut: TExtendedOut = extendedSchema.parse(extendedIn); // ✅ OK
```

I would also like to question the usefulness of the intersection of strictObjects, in the way that it is implemented currently. Although it may seem to make sense that if the first object does not allow extra properties, that the intersection with any other object will respect that, it is not how the typescript type system implements it. And especially the 'and' convenience method is misleading, since it reminds people (me at least) of `&` in typescript, but `{ a: ... } & { b: ... }` in typescript is equivalent to { a: ...; b: ... }, whereas `z.strictObject({a: ... }).and(z.object({ b: ... })` is equivalent to input type { [key: string]: never; }.
Also `z.object({ a: ... })` produces input type `{a: ...}` which is incorrect because it allows extra properties in the input (`{a: ...; [string]: any }` would be closer to the truth).

The choice seems to be dictated by ease-of-implementation (just check both schema's independently) rather than usefulness for a specific purpose.

