I recently found out that Zod unions are order dependent and this can lead to unexpected data loss if you parse with a union.  Is this intended?  It is dangerous in my opinion.

Here is a reproduction: https://github.com/bhouston/zod-unions-order-dependent

The problem is a union like this:

```tsx
const unionSchema = z.union([
    z.object({
        orgName: z.string(),
        role: z.enum(FileRole).optional(),
    }),
    z.object({
        orgName: z.string(),
        projectName: z.string(),
        role: z.enum(FileRole).optional(),
    }),
    z.object({
        orgName: z.string(),
        projectName: z.string(),
        assetName: z.string(),
        role: z.enum(FileRole).optional(),
    }),
]);
```

But when I try to parse this data, which matches with the last subschema of the union:

```tsx
const data = {
    orgName: 'orgName',
    projectName: 'projectName',
    assetName: 'assetName',
    role: FileRole.AVATAR,
}

const result = unionSchema.parse(data);
```

I get this result:
```json
{ orgName: 'orgName', role: 'AVATAR' }
```

It seems that unions do not try to maximize the amount of data that passesthrough, rather they use first match, even if it is lossy, is chosen and then it strips the data that doesn't match that first match.

This was very unexpected behaviour when I ran into it.  It isn't what I expected.

I have found that I can fix it with upgrading to "strictObject" or changing the order in which I declare the union so the richer definitions are first.

