  persons: z.array(
    z.object({
      id: z.string().min(1),
      name: z.string().min(1),
    })
  )
})
````

Why does this Infer to:
```
type Person = z.infer<typeof schema.shape.persons.element>;
```

results into
```
type Person = {
    id?: string;
    name?: string;
}
```

Why are the id and name possible undefined here? I would expect the type to be:
```
type Person = {
    id: string;
    name: string;
}
```

`z.array().nonempty()` no longer infers tuple types (`[T, ...T[]]`) since Zod v4.
Instead it resolves to a plain array (`T[]`).

This breaks type safety because the guarantee of at least one element is no longer reflected at the type level.

---

**Reproduction**

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

const schema = z.number().array().nonempty();
type NonEmptyArray = z.input<typeof schema>;

// Until v3.25.76
// type NonEmptyArray = [number, ...number[]]

// Starting from v4.0.0
// type NonEmptyArray = number[]
```

Codecs solve or make easier a lot of use-cases and help in putting complexity where it makes sense. The experience is already quite nice with "field-level" codecs but currently there is no nice way of extending "object" codecs.

As an example, let's take the following scenario.

```typescript
const User = z.object({
  id: z.uuid(),
  email: z.email(),
  name: z.string(),
});

const Post = z.codec(
  z.object({
    id: z.uuid(),
    authorId: z.uuid(),

    title: z.string(),
    content: z.string(),
  }),
  z.object({
    id: z.uuid(),
    author: z.object({ id: z.uuid() }),

    title: z.string(),
    content: z.string(),
  }),
  {
    decode: (v) => ({ ...v, author: { id: v.authorId } }),
    encode: (v) => ({ ...v, authorId: v.author.id }),
  }
);
```

This is nice because this allows you to easily transform and validate both sides of a layer boundary (network. repo/domain, etc.) and not have to maintain two separate schemas that can easily get out of sync. But if you look closely, most of the input/output schemas are the same. The difference is basically just one field (`creatorId` -> `creator.id` and vice versa). Currently, it doesn't seem to be possible to compose this. So if now you had another entity `Comment`, you'd need to write the same thing all over

```typescript
const Comment = z.codec(
  z.object({
    id: z.uuid(),
    postId: z.uuid(),
    authorId: z.uuid(),
    // ... other fields
  }),
  z.object({
    id: z.uuid(),
    post: z.object({ id: z.uuid() }),
    author: z.object({ id: z.uuid() }),

    // ... other fields
  }),
  {
    decode: (v) => ({
      ...v,
      post: { id: v.postId },
      author: { id: v.authorId },
    }),
    encode: (v) => ({
      ...v,
      postId: v.post.id,
      authorId: v.author.id,
    }),
  },
);

```

Not entirely a deal-breaker, but wouldn't it be nice if we could do something like

```typescript
const authorIdToIdObject = z.codec(
