### Context: 

To better understand the proposal, let's consider a case where we have a schema for validating sorting options, such as:

```ts
const schema = z.object({
  sortBy: z.enum(["title", "email"]).optional().default("title"),
  sortOrder: z.enum(["asc", "desc"]).optional().default("asc"),
});
```

This schema can be easily validated using `.parse()`; `.safeParse()`; `.parseAsync()`; or `.safeParseAsync()`. In either case, errors can be handled according to the required logic, such as throwing an exception or setting the parameters to default values. This proposal is about the setting default params part.

### Proposal: 

The `.fallback()` method can solve this issue. It works like a try/catch wrapper that accepts a value or a function, which will be re-executed whenever an error occurs: 

Example 1: 

```ts
const schema = z.object({
  sortBy: z.enum(["title", "email"]).optional().default("title").fallback("title"),
  sortOrder: z.enum(["asc", "desc"]).optional().default("asc").fallback("asc"),
});

const result = schema.parse({ sortBy: "some-value-1", sortOrder: "some-value-2" });

console.log(result) // ✅ Valid: { sortBy: "title", sortOrder: "asc" }
```

Example 2: 

```ts
const schema = z.object({
  sortBy: z.enum(["title", "email"]).optional().default("title").fallback("title"),
  sortOrder: z.enum(["asc", "desc"]).optional().default("asc"),
});

const result = schema.parse({ sortBy: "some-value-1", sortOrder: "some-value-2" });

console.log(result) // ❌ Validation failed: [ { code: 'invalid_enum_value', ... } ]
```

