Proposal related to https://github.com/colinhacks/zod/issues/5143#issuecomment-3218366995

> Wow OpenAPI 3.0 is even worse than I thought 🫠 [...]. Definitely happy to have more tests here.

---

To avoid regression or oversights that might lead to invalid output (like #5146),
I would like to propose an addition to the `openapi-3.0` `toJSONSchema`  `target` tests:

How about building a custom matcher to validate this function’s output against the OpenAPI specification?  

I’ve used [@seriousme/openapi-schema-validator](https://github.com/seriousme/openapi-schema-validator) before to validate 3.x OpenAPI schemas,
and I think it could be really handy for creating a custom `vitest` matcher.

<details><summary>Proposal for a custom matcher</summary>
<p>

```ts
import { Validator } from '@seriousme/openapi-schema-validator';
import { expect } from 'vitest';

interface CustomMatchers<R = unknown> {
  toBeValidInsideOpenAPISchema30: () => R;
}

declare module 'vitest' {
  interface Assertion<T = any> extends CustomMatchers<T> {}
  interface AsymmetricMatchersContaining extends CustomMatchers {}
}

const validator = new Validator();

expect.extend({
  async toBeValidInsideOpenAPISchema30(
    this: { isNot: boolean },
    zodSchema: $ZodType,
  ): Promise<{ pass: boolean; message: () => string }> {
    const res = await validator.validate({
      openapi: '3.0.0',
      // ...
      components: { schemas: { test: zodSchema } }
    });

    const messages = [
      `OpenAPI schema is${this.isNot ? '' : ' not'} valid against ${validator.version}`,
    ];

    if (!res.valid) {
      messages.push(JSON.stringify(res.errors, null, 2));
    }

    return {
      pass: res.valid,
      message: () => messages.join('\n'),
    };
  },
});

```
</p>
</details> 

Using this custom matcher, we can ensure the schema is actually valid,  
rather than relying only on string snapshots,
which sometimes can be generated incorrectly (as happened to me earlier).

Personally, I like to use both snapshots and validation.  
Having an inline or file snapshot alongside a validator as a reference is really useful, IMHO.

