Here is the issue i have discovered in one of my projects:

On very deeply nested zod object schema definitions, it can happen that the object's type cannot be inferred anymore. This is because the maximum length the typescript compiler can serialize is being reached.

I fixed this issue by giving every zod object a type hint using `z.ZodType<SomeType>`, which is clearly not the cleanest way. Because now the actual type is first of all not shown properly anymore, and second of all I cannot use functions like `.extend()` anymore because it is not recognized as an object anymore. The type becomes something like this:
```ts
const SomeSchema: z.ZodType<SomeType, unknown, z.core.$ZodTypeInternals<SomeType, unknown>>
```

Here is how I created one of the objects:
```ts
export type SomeType = {
  'field1': Field1Type;
  'field2': Field2Type;
};

export const SomeSchema: z.ZodType<SomeType> = z.strictObject({
  'field1': Field1Schema,
  'field2': Field2Schema,
});
```

Creating the typescript types itself and nesting them like the zod schemas work perfectly fine. So my guess is that the problem is how the zod schema types are represented. For each schema definition, always the whole representation is inferred. So when referencing a zod schema inside another schema, the type is destructered and added to the root schema. Typescript on the other hand stops the recursion and the type becomes more simple and robust.

Short example:
```ts
export type Field1Type = {
  'field1.1': string;
  'field1.2': number;
};

export type Field2Type = {
  'field2.1': boolean;
  'field2.2': Date;
};

export type SomeType = {
  field1: Field1Type;
  field2: Field2Type;
};

export const Field1Schema = z.strictObject({
  'field1.1': z.string(),
  'field1.2': z.number(),
});

export const Field2Schema = z.strictObject({
  'field2.1': z.boolean(),
  'field2.2': z.date(),
});

export const SomeSchema = z.strictObject({
  field1: Field1Schema,
  field2: Field2Schema,
});
```

This gives the following type hint for `SomeType`:
```ts
type SomeType = {
    field1: Field1Type;
    field2: Field2Type;
}
```

And the schema type hint is the following for `SomeSchema`:
```ts
const SomeSchema: z.ZodObject<{
    field1: z.ZodObject<{
        'field1.1': z.ZodString;
        'field1.2': z.ZodNumber;
    }, z.core.$strict>;
    field2: z.ZodObject<{
        'field2.1': z.ZodBoolean;
        'field2.2': z.ZodDate;
    }, z.core.$strict>;
}, z.core.$strict>
```

I think it would be better if there would be a way to represent the schema more like the following (I clearly don't know how the internal stuff is handled, so this will not be working in any way, but should serve as an example of how it could look like):
```ts
const SomeSchema: z.ZodObject<{
    field1: z.ZodObject<Field1Schema>;
    field2: z.ZodObject<Field2Schema>;
}, z.core.$strict>
```

Is this something that would be possible and is maybe planned to change in the future or will it stay like this?

Any insights would be appreciated.

### Environment

- Typescript: 5.8.3
- Zod: 4.0.14

